Widen the nova_scheduler healthcheck.

nova_scheduler's healthcheck is "healthcheck_port nova-scheduler
5672", which asserts an established AMQP connection. Since Nova
33.0.0 (2026.1) the scheduler, conductor and compute managers each
sleep for [DEFAULT]/manager_shutdown_timeout, 160 seconds by
default, inside their SIGTERM handler. Nova's release note for that
feature tells operators to widen their supervision timeouts to
suit:

  The default value of [DEFAULT]/graceful_shutdown_timeout has been
  changed from 60 to 180 seconds for all Nova services. [...]
  Operators using external system (e.g. k8s, systemd) to manage the
  Nova services should ensure that their service stop timeouts are
  set to at least graceful_shutdown_timeout to avoid forcefully
  killing service before Nova finish its graceful shutdown.

Kolla-Ansible supervises Nova with container healthchecks rather
than systemd, and those were never widened to match. The default 30
second interval with 3 retries tolerates only 90 seconds, so
whether check-failure.sh trips comes down to where its poll lands
in the restart window. From a rocky-10 job on 2026-08-03:

  01:38:55.303 Caught SIGTERM signal, graceful exiting of service
  01:38:56.787 service stopped RPC server on topic: scheduler
  01:38:56.787 Scheduler service manager is waiting for 160 seconds
               to finish in-progress tasks
  01:41:36.787 manager graceful shutdown finished
  01:41:36.850 Starting scheduler node (version 33.1.0)

The post-deploy sanity check ran at 01:40:53, inside that window,
and the job failed. Across a trailing 30 days this shows up in 328
Kolla CI builds and is fatal to 118 of them, across 13 job variants
and spread evenly over rocky-10, ubuntu-noble and debian-trixie.
Every one of those is on master or stable/2026.1 and none is on an
older branch, which is exactly where the Nova change landed.

Raise nova_scheduler_healthcheck_retries from
default_container_healthcheck_retries to 8, which gives 240 seconds
and comfortably clears the window. Services already accept
per-service healthcheck overrides (<service>_healthcheck_<option>),
so this is a change of default rather than of code. Document it in
the sample globals.yml alongside the defaults it overrides, and add
a release note: existing deployments pick the wider healthcheck up
when the container is next recreated. This follows 795fbb104, which
widened mariadb_monitor_galera_healthcheck_timeout for the same
class of problem.

The scheduler is the only Nova service that needs this, and the
reason is how Kolla-Ansible restarts it rather than anything
particular to the scheduler manager:

* nova-scheduler is signalled in place: refresh_scheduler_cell_cache
  sends SIGHUP to the container's PID on every deploy and every
  reconfigure, oslo.service's ProcessLauncher turns that into
  SIGTERM to its children, and the whole 160 second wait then
  elapses with nothing to cut it short.

* nova-conductor and nova-super-conductor wait for the same period
  in ConductorManager.graceful_shutdown(), but are only ever
  stopped by the container engine, which SIGKILLs them after
  docker_graceful_timeout, 60 seconds by default. That plus the
  container start stays inside the 90 seconds the default
  healthcheck covers.

* nova-compute waits too, but nova/cmd/compute.py is the only
  caller passing topic_alt, so compute holds a second RPC server --
  and so an established AMQP connection -- open for the whole wait,
  and its healthcheck never goes red.

Scanning 357 master and stable/2026.1 Kolla builds from the last
ten days for every "Discovered unhealthy container" line -- every
build where nova_scheduler was flagged, plus a one in eight sample
of the rest, including the upgrade jobs that do restart conductor
and compute -- nova_scheduler is the only container ever reported.
The conductors' margin is thinner than it looks though, and it
comes from docker_graceful_timeout rather than from anything in
Nova, so the sample globals.yml and the release note point at
nova_conductor_healthcheck_retries for deployments that raise
docker_graceful_timeout towards manager_shutdown_timeout.

Nova describes the feature as experimental and plans to replace the
fixed sleep with real task tracking (blueprint
nova-services-graceful-shutdown-part2-task-tracking, currently in
review as I2de2172b5836f156c2154ca73cbff558ec587ea3). Until that
lands the wait is unconditional, and for nova_scheduler it is dead
time: nova/service.py stops and drains the RPC server before
calling the manager's graceful_shutdown(). This override should be
dropped once the Nova side is fixed.

Prompt: Michael asked for assistance debugging why CI jobs were
failing with nova-scheduler timeouts. We traced the delay to Nova's
manager_shutdown_timeout, and confirming 2026.1 shipped that
behaviour, produced the citations above. Michael then asked whether
the healthcheck needed widening for the other Nova services too,
which produced the per-service analysis and the CI log scan.

